        "7.3 Metrics you should calculate in your own experiment\nTrack more than total sales.\n| Metric | Formula | Why it matters |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Pin impressions | Pinterest-reported impressions | Measures how often Pins were shown |\n| Pin outbound clicks | Pinterest-reported outbound clicks | Measures visits sent to the store |\n| Click-through rate | Outbound clicks ÷ impressions × 100 | Shows whether the creative and topic attract clicks |\n| Product-page conversion rate | Paid orders ÷ product-page visits × 100 | Shows whether the listing converts visitors |\n| Freebie download rate | Free downloads ÷ free-page visits × 100 | Measures the free offer |\n| Free-to-paid conversion | Paid purchases attributed to freebie users ÷ freebie users × 100 | Measures the upgrade funnel, if attribution is available |\n| Gross product revenue | Sum of paid product sales | Measures sales before costs |\n| Net proceeds | Gross revenue − platform/payment fees − refunds − other costs | Shows what remains before tax |\n| Refund rate | Refunds ÷ paid orders × 100 | Helps identify product or expectation problems |",
        "Do not calculate conversion rates without the correct denominator. Seven paid orders out of 24 total reported orders/downloads is not the same as a conversion rate from Pinterest visitors. The experiment does not provide total impressions, outbound clicks, product-page visits, or unique visitors.",
        "7.4 What remains unknown\nThe transcript does not specify:\n- Total Pinterest impressions.\n- Total outbound clicks.\n- Number of unique product-page visitors.\n- Whether all 17 free downloads came from Pinterest.\n- How many paid buyers previously downloaded the freebie.\n- Total Gumroad and payment-processing fees.\n- Refunds or chargebacks.\n- Currency conversion and withdrawal costs.\n- Exact net profit.\n- The number of Pins published for each product.\n- Whether results continued after the seven-day test.\nThese are not minor details. They are necessary to understand profitability and repeatability. Collect them in your own test rather than filling the gaps with assumptions."
